Date: Mon, 6 Sep 93 20:46:21 -0700
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: PHIL: Ethics, "Green Goo", human population

Ray Cromwell replies to my downloaded-into-squirrel aspect (slightly reordered
in the interests of a more coherent reply):

>   I propose (quite arbitrarily) we simply draw the line at humans. [...]
>
>   You might argue that there is a middle-ground. If so, I challenge you
> to rationally calculate the amount of "allowable killing and murdering" of
> the environment we are entitled too then. [...]

    How satisfying to have clear identification of points of disagreement!
Ray's position is of course frequently encountered:  Much of traditional law,
mores, ethics and attitudes is predicated upon what is in a nutshell (sorry,
we squirrels think like that) a boolean distinction between humans and non-
humans, with a general trend toward including most adult and many juvenile
members of _Homo_sapiens_ in the former category (though there have of course
been some historically notable waffles in the definition).
    At risk of finding myself out on a limb (sorry once again...), I do not
personally consider a boolean distinction appropriate.  (Relevant sidebar
toward a currently running Krieger/Wilson thread on ethical premises and
their origins:  I agree with Krieger that ethical premises seem to be entirely
matters of personal choice -- thus I wouldn't dream of trying to tell Ray
that he is "wrong" in any absolute sense -- but nonetheless, my position
seems a lot like Wilson's.)  Ahem, I do not personally consider a boolean
distinction appropriate, and since we seem to meta-agree that ethical premises
are personal, I can't really hope to "explain" why, but perhaps it will help
to elaborate that I seem to sense substantial similarities in physiology,
psychology and behavior between _H._sapiens_ and diverse other animal
species, and I seem to encounter serious cognitive dissonance and moderate
guilt at assigning a large dollop of "rights" to humans and none at all to
such interesting, personable and companionable creatures as us squirrels.
    A difficult corollary to this position is suggested by Ray's Puckish
challenge:  One advantage of booleans in ethics is that they make it easy
to make decisions -- thus if the world is divided into *them* and *us*,
then perhaps I have to worry about *us* but at least *they* don't count at
all.  (Which is just as well, because *we* can't count -- being squirrels.)
For lack of booleans, I must in principle be prepared to quantify what I
mean by "human", in the event of a conflict of "rights" between two
different species.
    Note in passing that the issue is not strictly one that occurs solely
at the interface between species, for as I mentioned above, various societies
have at various times denied some or all "rights" to many persons who are
undoubtedly genetic members of _H._sapiens_, such as children, senile adults,
women, racial and ethnic minorities, convicted felons, persons with severe
brain trauma, and college students (who may perhaps be subsumed into one or
more of the preceding categories, and no fair asking which one I had in
mind).  Some of the social attitudes toward such "partially human" members
of _H._sapiens_ might provide interesting ideas for dealing with other
species.
    End of note.  To continue, I have no perfect algorithm for quantification
of "human" -- algorithmic ethics seems to be a very difficult subject in
general.  Thus I will not attempt to accept Ray's challenge because (a) I
would lose, and (b) we would then likely end up just debating premises in
the algorithm, if I had one.  But lack of an algorithm doesn't mean that
I am unwilling to make decisions based on an implicit quantification, or
that I consider it unimportant to make such decisions; it merely means that
I worry about them a lot.  Fortunately for my peace of mind, I haven't had
to make them too often -- most "real" ones work out to taking exceptionally
good care of pets (I think -- since the pets are not yet uploaded it is
difficult to ask them how well I am doing), feeding the possums in the back
yard (ditto), working at Wildlife Rescue, and at least trying to be gentle
with the environment.
    Restating that I am merely attempting to articulate my own position, not
to convince anybody of anything, I close --

                            -- Jay Freeman, F.E.S. (*)
                               the other Jay,
                               the other Freeman.

(*) First Extropian Squirrel

Appendix:  To illustrate a possible operational approach to a quantitative
           definition of "human", here is another thought experiment:

    It's time to prepare plentiful pounds of processed protein for the
next extropaganza.  You are in charge, and you have exactly two choices of
what to run through the meat grinder -- I mean, the protein pulverizer.
You must choose one of the two choices (there, there, it's only a thought
experiment):

      Choice #1:  M individuals of species A.
      Choice #2:  N individuals of species B.

    Set "species A" to _H._sapiens_, "species B" to whatever you like
(or don't like) (no comment as to whether I am referring to taste or company
in using the word "like").  Now, what proportion must M and N be in to
make the choice difficult?  Possible interesting complication:  Suppose
that one or both of M and N is "significantly large" (?) compared to the
total extant population of the species in question.

