Date: Tue,  7 Sep 1993 01:33:05 -0400 (EDT)
From: Christina M Grimes <cg39+@andrew.cmu.edu>
Subject: PHIL: Ethics etc.

Hi, everyone. As my first leap into extropian debate, I thought I'd add
another view to the animal/human rights question... (as if we needed
another one 7:)

Arguing over whom (or what) to give "rights" would, methinks, require an
agreed-upon definition. There may be one, and I just missed it (a
distinct possibility), but I would propose something of the order: "A
(for lack of a better word) prerogative that a being is said to hold in
regard to vital primaries of existence, i.e. life, property, freedom,
etc., that is dependent on that being's understanding of and protection
of those individual values, and, as a result of understanding these
values, respect for such rights in other beings." 

A "right" is a kind of social contract; it is a recognition that there
are others similar to you that should be treated like you treat
yourself. If you extend a right to someone, you assume that right will
be granted to you in return. To grant a right to something that doesn't
understand or care about rights, such as a hungry shark, is a little
pointless.

According to this, an animal--like Jay Freeman's squirrel (though not an
uploaded superintelligence!)--would "understand" the right to
existence/life, because it knows enough to protect it, but only on a
very basic and instinctual level, and not enough to respect any kind of
"right" to life in other squirrels, humans, or nuts. It protects itself
out of programmed reflex, and refrains from attacking other squirrels
because it is not selectively advantageous--i.e., risks getting killed
in defense, and harms the species as a whole.

This is not to say that a squirrel does not have a right to existence.
We are not necessarily entitled to slash squirrel throats for fun just
because they're not human. A squirrel has a kind of semi-right to
existence, one that it protects but does not understand enough to apply
to itself and others (and other species), placing it somewhere between a
rock and a kind of being that can fully understand and implement rights.
The semi-right of the squirrel to exist, however, is overrided when it
is, say, found to be rabid, and poses a threat to one's children or pets.

Not all Homo sapiens are human, either. Vegetables, senile adults,
severely insane or brain damaged people, young children, and fetuses
(and college students, someone said? ha, ha.) all apply. A fetus, while
unborn, has a semi-right to existence, unless the mother doesn't want a
humanoid thing growing in her uterus and feeding off her blood, at which
time its semi-right is overrided (since it can't exist on its own,
whereas an infant can be given away.) A young child doesn't understand
property, and so must be given property, and have it defended by, the
parents; and likewise the child is wont to snatch things from playmates,
with no regard for the fact that it's *their* favorite blanky. 

To a certain extent, rights do depend on what a society defines them to
be. As Mr. Freeman pointed out, many societies have denied rights to
such groups as slaves, women, red-haired people, black people, etc. In
an anarchy (or other such system), however, rights become a contract
between individuals, usually implicit. In that case, if someone feels
all chickens deserve human rights, well, fine, se can treat them as
such; protect them, give them property, etc. However, they certainly
won't return the courtesy, which is the essence of what a right is.

Comments?

Ratha                                      

