Date: Tue, 7 Sep 93 0:14:00 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: ETHICS: The Calculus of Valuing Lives

"Offer: Will trade the lives of one million Bengali peasants for the
lives of 100 tigers. Tigers must be delivered in good health. Contact
me at my BlackNet address."

Jay Freeman presents us with a "thought experiment" by which we can
quantize (perhaps) our levels of caring for various species:

> Appendix: To illustrate a possible operational approach to a quantitative 
> definition of "human", here is another thought experiment: 
> 
> It's time to prepare plentiful pounds of processed protein for the 
> next extropaganza.  You are in charge, and you have exactly two choices of 
> what to run through the meat grinder -- I mean, the protein pulverizer.  
> You must choose one of the two choices (there, there, it's only a thought 
> experiment): 
> 
> Choice #1: M individuals of species A.  
> Choice #2: N individuals of species B.  
> 
> Set "species A" to _H._sapiens_, "species B" to whatever you like 
> (or don't like) (no comment as to whether I am referring to taste or company 
> in using the word "like").  Now, what proportion must M and N be in to 
> make the choice difficult?  Possible interesting complication: Suppose 
> that one or both of M and N is "significantly large" (?) compared to the 
> total extant population ofthe species in question.

Jay and I had a similar discussion when he was visiting Santa Cruz one
day, some months back. 

I admitted that, despite my libertarian, even Randish, views on many
subjects, I would almost certainly choose to save the last 100 Bengal tigers
over, say, saving some large number of Third Worlders. I think the
figure mentioned was a million Bengalis living in the flood plains.
Bengalis for Bengalis, so to speak.

In a world of 5,500 million people, 3,000 million of them living at
near the subsistence level, who cares if a million fewer are here
tomorrow? Well, _they_ care, and maybe their closest relatives care,
but not many others. ("350,000 die in monsoons. But, first, the latest
on the rescue of Baby Jessica from that well in Texas...")

More to the point, _I_ don't care about these starving millions, nor
do I expect them to care about me. How I spend my money, as Robin
Hanson tells us in every post he writes, reveals how I place values on
things and how I make estimates of the odds and the relative
importance of things. I choose to spend $400 or more a year on my two
cats than send this money to Bangladesh or Sudan to buy life-saving
vaccines and food. By some figures I have heard recently, $400 a year
would "save" about a dozen of these starving folks...save them
essentially permanently, possibly even saving them enough so they
could start their own (very) small business: sending a small cart to
Bangladesh to a family would allow a family to become "traders" and
thus climb out of abject poverty ("60 Minutes" had just such a report,
on how private bankers are--hooray!--making these very small loans
that allow these peasants to make it up from the brink.)

Thus, in the "calculus of lives," I value my two cats at about 6
peasants.

And then there's my CD collection. And my health club membership. And
the movies I see. And the Worldcon I just attended. Well, you all see
what I'm getting at. This calculus problem has a solution that's very
clear to me.

(I mean no offense to Bengalis or other "people of color." They're just
the folks furthest away from me, geographically and culturally. In
fact, I'd probably make the same trade for most of my local neighbors.
So there.)

I'd probably chicken out if I had to "pull a lever" to send the
Bengalis to their death and save the tigers, as this "direct action"
invokes the usual moral responses, humanity memes, etc. "Pulling a
lever" sound dangerously close to "initiating force," a moral no-no to
libertarians, but the "lever" can of course be wired up to choose
between" "Send life-saving drugs to Bengalis" or "Purchase land for
habitats and zoos for tigers." 

(I did not take Jay's question to call for initiating force, by
actively killing folks. Rather, choosing between actions that have the
same final effect. Same difference, in a deep sense, but to many the
acts of commission are of greater moral significance than the acts of
omission. The standard libertarian/Randite argument goes that no
person has the "right" to the life-saving drugs or food I may have,
whereas they do have a "right" not be shot in the head by me, unless
in defense. There's a whole bundle of ideas here, which I won't
digress into.)

But make no mistake about it, many of us "value" human lives at a lot
less than we value animal lives, as evidenced by our contributions to
wildlife preservation efforts, to the care and feeding of our pets,
and so on. What many of us spend on our individual dog and cat pets
for veterinary care and Fancy Feast would almost certainly "save" the
lives of some families who are living close to the margin of survival
and cannot afford certain medicines, food, or shelter.

There are undoubtedly curves showing how many people are dying daily
for lack of various amounts of money: some are dying for lack of a few
_pennies_ (vaccines, vitamins, basic food fall into this category),
some for _dollars_ (medical treatment, food), fewer for kilobucks
(special operations), fewer still for small fortunes (expensive
operations), and a very few are dying for lack of immense fortunes
(the $100 million per life saved for the Siamese twin case comes to
mind).  Please, no flames that this is a socialist rant! I am not
arguing that money _should_ be spent on these things, or that public
charity and subsidies is a good thing in the long run. Rather, I am
just making the point that any of us could get on a plane to Dacca,
Bangladesh with our pockets stuffed with dollars and probably _save_
the lives, at least for a few months and perhaps longer, of many of
these folks now dying on the margin. I have not chose to do this with
any of my money, whereas I _have_ donated to zoos and wildlife funds,
thus making my point about a calculus of human lives.

By where and how we spend our own money we show how we value lives.

This may be distasteful to some, to talk about saving tigers over
starving Bengalis (and I haven't gotten into why I personally favor
this saving of tigers qua tigers....the beauty of this approach is
that I don't _have_ to justify why I value lives this way), but to me
it's a very Extropian way of looking at things. A market view. No
sanctimonious blather about "the sanctity of human life" when our
actions tell the real story.


-Tim May


