or political groups set the rates? China tells Americans?

Besides, what's the "right" population? Is Hong Kong overcrowded or
not? (It is for _me_, but apparently not for a lot of others.)

If I have the economic means to take care of a family of more than the
approved "replacement level" (or less, as in the new movie
"Fortress"), why should others care? And if an entire nation, like
Bangladesh (which I picked on in my last posting) has families in it
which tend to breed up to very high levels, why should I care? As Jay
Leno used to say, "Don't worry about a million starving peasants,
they'll make more."

In line with my recent posting on a "Calculus for Valuing Lives," I
say: "Let 'em breed!" But if some family is too large to support
itself adequately, they just starve. Life is tough. (Some flavors of
libertarians favor some form of intervention to save the children,
arguing that the children did not choose to live in squalor and
starvation, etc. I cannot condone intervention in the lives of
families, whether it's to control their television watching or to
force special diets upon them. Parents control their children, up to
some age of majority and the children can damn well starve along with
their parents. Sounds harsh, and it is, but the alternative is worse.)

Anyway, I don't want to preach to the choir, here. Lots of flaws in
the Paul Ehrlich model of "zero population growth." I suggest the
Julian Simon article in the latest "Extropy," ironically enough.

Economic development tends to reduce the incentives and social
pressures for very large families, resulting in the so-called
"demographic transition," whereby developed countries tend toward a
very slow population growth.

And resources are not in short supply (historically low prices),
plenty of arable land remains untilled, advances in crop growth and
hydroponics will supply plenty of food, and waste disposal is not one
tenth the problem people think it is. The real problem remains
oppressive government planning in many countries, cutting off the
natural development cycles which can trigger the demographic
transition.

Controlling government beats controlling population levels.

-Tim May


