From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: NANO: Transition speed & political control

Jamie Dinkelacker and Eric Drexler both make excellent comments
on the speed of nanotech transition.  While discussing political
implications, Drexler makes the technological case for rapid transition:

> There are three reasons for this: (1) the
> historically-unprecedented availability of computer simulations and
> computer aided design techniques, which will enable designs to precede tool
> development, (2) the sheer speed of experimentation possible with fast
> manufacturing processes, and (3) the ease with which a poor design based on
> (for example) diamond-fiber composites and nanocomputers can beat a good
> design based on conventional materials and devices. 

Depending on the product, (3) can be very limited.  For example, diamond
does not provide the best tensile strength, which is important to many
products.  Overall, materials improvements in engineering qualities
like strength, stiffness, etc.  etc. will tend to be less than an order 
of magnitude.  (2) applies mostly to the smaller, higher-frequency 
regimes of nanotechnology, much less to bulk products and large
structures.  (1) may be a barrier to rapid transition 
if computer-aided design causes physical design paradigms to change 
with the speed of programming languages.  

There will likely exist many high-frequency partial assemblers well
before the first painfully slow general assembler.  A completely
general assembler, capable of producing _any_ product made of
any possible materials, may not even be feasible.  Living things 
provide existence proof for self-replication within a larger ecosystem, 
but not a proof of the possibility of rapid general manufacturing
of a wide range of products.  The ability to make everything
out of diamond is neat, but many products will use materials
superior to diamond for the purpose at hand, and thus be
assembled by nanomachines designed specially to handle those
materials.  

Most, if not all, of the tasks envisioned for nanotech can be
performed without general-purpose machines or complete mechanical
self-replication.  For example, cell repair nanomachines with designs
focused on the task at hand will likely work better than
nanomachines that devote most of their resources to general-purpose
manufacture or self-replication.  Design automation accelerates this
trend: for example silicon compilers allow the manufacture of
customized circuitry in small batches, instead of mass-production
of identical designs to be customized later.

Thus, I see a more incremental transition -- from advanced 
nanolithography (producing much of the computational speedup
envsioned for nanotech) and biotech (performing many of the jobs now 
envisioned for cell repair machines) to special-purpose nanomachines
for cell repair, industrial tasks, etc.   Even AI and/or uploading
might occur during this period, and progress will already be
quite rapid without fully self-replicating nanomachines.  Then
self-replicators come on-line.  At first they will be very slow
and product only a narrow range of products; they might be
used for tasks like space colonization where industrial expansion
is more important than production efficiency, quality, variety,
etc.  By this time, manufacturing productivity increases will already
have been vast, and manufacturing as a fraction of GDP will likely
be tiny; less than agriculture today.  Services (esp. medicine
and entertainment) dominate.  At that point rapid self-replicating
machines appear, but by that time they don't make a big difference.

At no point is there a rapid transition; rather there is a
slowly accelerating increase in manufacturing productivity combined
with a decrease in the importance of manufacturing _per se_, just
as agriculture is less important in developed countries today, because
relatively far easier and there are other things we want to do.
In the nanotech world those "other things"  will likely include life
extension, cryonics repair, entertainment, travel, etc.

Note also that the "gray goo" weapons depend on the most advanced
stages of nanotechnology, by which time our ability to think about
and deal with the problem will be vastly enhanced.

Digressing back to the issue of political control, 
design automation also decentralizes the design process.  Compilers
and program libraries allow computer programming to be performed by 
a larger group of people educated at the task at hand instead of a 
small elite well-verse in the arcana of machine language.  Silicon 
compilers allow a small studio to design sophisticated customized 
circuitry.  Assuming, contrary to what I've argued so far, a single 
Breakthrough that causes a rapid transition, the design software and
accompanying human knowledge and skills will be spread so far and wide 
that the chances of the Breakthrough occuring under the control of those 
who already dominate world politics is small.  

Even if The Breakthrough gives a major political power a head
start, its monopoly may not last long enough for even the brief time 
needed to capitalize on it.  Most of the knowledge and tools needed
to recreate that breakthrough will already be public knowledge, and
the remaining trade secrets may be very hard to keep in an
era when such secrets can be sold untraceably ("BlackNet").
(This is even more likely if the change is more incremental,
as I argued earlier).  The mere knowledge that such a breakthrough is 
possible provides motivation and clues for competitors.  Design-
ahead will give long term warning (here we are discussing 
it even now), and the news when it happens will be extremely difficult
to stifle.  Finally, while a large scale technology like nuclear power 
can be controlled (to some extent -- it took U.S.S.R. only 5 years to 
catch up) small-scale nanotech advances might be pulled off by small,
obscure labs out of the range or even knowledge of government
control.

