Subject: PHIL: Ethics etc.

Christina Grimes: 
> Hi, everyone. As my first leap into extropian debate, 

Welcome, and that was pretty good for a first post.  I like the
idea that the existence of a right depends both on the ability
to defend it and the recognition that said right exist in others
similar to oneself.

> [A squirrel] protects itself
> out of programmed reflex, and refrains from attacking other squirrels
> because it is not selectively advantageous--i.e., risks getting killed
> in defense, and harms the species as a whole.

There may also be some planning ahead involved in that defense.
And a nit, such behavior tends to to serve the propagation the 
squirrel's genes; the interest of the species as a whole is
irrelevent to the phenomenon.

The squirrel's refraining from attacking might well be a form of 
reciprocal altruism, in which case we have to ask, at what point does 
reciprocal altruism constitute a recognition of rights in others?
There's a subtle but important difference between "do unto others
as they have done unto you" (reciprocal altruism) and "do unto
others as you would have them do unto you" (a recognition of
the other's rights, in a sort of over-generalized way, but based on
confidence in the reciprocal altruism of the other).  That difference 
may just be planning -- and the squirrels might do that, too.

> This is not to say that a squirrel does not have a right to existence.

Sure it does.  We might not like killing squirrels, I know I 
felt awful when I ran over a squirrel earlier this summer, but 
that's a different issue.  "Right" implies some kind of tort can 
be applied to redress violation of that right, but I don't think anyone 
but the squirrel's "similars", if they knew how, should be able to sue or 
otherwise take revenge because I ran over the squirrel.  (Class action 
lawsuit with winnings converted to acorns?).

BTW do y'all realize how silly the concept of "animal rights" sounds
to the typical hunter or animal farmer?  Only we modern-day city
slickers have the luxury of getting all teary-eyed over Bambi.

I wonder what other "rights" we will conjure up in the future
because we have the luxury to do so.  Cute virtual 
characters seem to be a good candidate.  Many people already
get uptight when their favorite character is removed from a TV
series, for example.  Bambi herself is, amazingly enough, imaginary.
But imagine if Disney made a sequel that killed her off?

Perhaps the case might also be made for the right of old artifacts 
to retain their personality.  For example the right of an old 
historical building to remain standing, the right of "Casablanca" to 
never be colorized, etc.  Like animal rights, this implies 
a definition of rights not restricted to entitities who understand
the existence of said rights in ourselves.

> A young child doesn't understand
> property, and so must be given property, and have it defended by, the
> parents; 

I'm uncomfortable with the model where parents have an obligation
to their children, but not vice versa.  With such an imbalance,
and with society being so paranoid about how people raise their
children, why would any rational person choose to become a parent?
(And indeed, many choose not too -- but that's another thread!)
The whole issue of outsiders interfering with the family (in some 
cases justified, eg child abuse) is messy, alas.

